      "id": 1,
      "text": "Baldwin, Louisiana, takes its name from John Baldwin, an Ohio educator who founded what would later become Baldwin‑Wallace University.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baldwin%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Darby House, once owned by the Baldwin family, remains on the National Register of Historic Places after staying in the family until 1937.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baldwin%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1867, Baldwin acquired the Darby plantation, a sprawling 1,700‑acre estate that helped shape the town’s early economy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baldwin%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Gilbert Academy and Industrial School once operated in Baldwin, reflecting the town’s early commitment to vocational education.",
